  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\frac{1}{4})+{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{1}{4})(x-\\frac{1}{4})+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{1}{4})}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{1}{4})}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{1}{4})=-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4})=-{\\pi }^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{1}{4})}{2} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{2})}{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
